  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1216 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nWednesday's cold front has finally cleared the Mid-South with\ncooler dewpoints (in the low to mid 70s) filtering in behind it.\nHighs today will be mostly in the low 90s with heat indices\ntopping out at around 100F. The Delta Region, our hotspot where\ndewpoints will likely linger in the mid 70s this afternoon, has\nthe potential to reach 105+F today, however, confidence was not\nquite high enough in moisture pulling to issue a Heat Advisory in\nthis area. We're also looking largely dry, though, primarily \ndiurnally driven, pop-up showers over north Mississippi can't be \nruled out this afternoon as PWATs remain around 1.8\" in this area.\n\nOur dry spell will be very short-lived as by tomorrow a\npositively tilted trough will begin to push east into the Upper\nMississippi Valley from the Midwest. This trough will also push\nus, here in the Mid-South, further into a northwest flow regime\naloft. As this system begins to push in, forecast PWATs begin to\nsurge to 2.2\"+, nearing the 99th percentile for this time of the\nyear. Shower and thunderstorm chances will begin to increase west\nof the Mississippi River by the early afternoon hours. An\nattendant cold front will also extend from the aforementioned\ntrough and begin to influence the Mid-South by tomorrow evening.\nShower and thunderstorm chances will become numerous by the\nevening hours. Ahead of this front and upper-trough, a 30-40 kt\nlow-level jet will begin to kick in, opening a window for a very\nconditional threat for a few strong to severe storms in the\nafternoon to early evening hours. Digging into the parameter\nspacing for this threat, forecast soundings are hinting at around\n1500 J/kg of SBCAPE, 25 kts of effective shear, DCAPE of around\n500, mid-level lapse rates at around 5.7 C/km, and dewpoints in\nthe mid 70s. Precipitation may not line up with this environment,\nhowever, if storms are able to dip into this marginal instability\nthey could have enough energy to form a tall enough updraft to\nproduce damaging winds. However, the overall confidence in this\nthreat remains very low.\n\nWith increased moisture comes increased dewpoints. As dewpoints\nincrease tomorrow, heat indices in the Delta Region are expected\nto exceed 105F. As such, a Heat Advisory has been issued for this\nregion valid from 11AM through 8PM. NBM and HREF probabilities\nare around 50% of exceeding 105F north and east of this advisory\nresulting in a potential extension in the next forecast issuance.\nDespite yesterday's cold front, summer heat continues to linger.\n\nAs the aforementioned cold front edges closer to the Mid-South in\ncombination with surging PWATs, by late Friday into Saturday, a\nlocalized heavy rainfall may exist. Though, forecast rainfall\namounts are equating to less than an inch across our area,\nlocalized higher amounts are anticipated. Confidence in the\noverall flash flooding threat overnight Saturday remains <30%,\nbut remains something to keep an eye on given our environment.\n\nBehind Saturday's cold front, cooler air will filter in leading\nto a pleasant August day Sunday with highs in the 80s. A few\nscattered showers will linger mainly along the Tennessee River\nSunday as our area continues to be influenced by troughing over\nthe southern Ohio River Valley. By mid-week, we'll begin to\nslowly warm back up as ensembles are hinting at the\naforementioned trough pushing further east and ridging slowly\npushing into our area from the west. However, dewpoints look to\nremain in the low to mid 70s, hopefully keeping humidity levels\nat bay as temperatures begin to increase into the latter half of\nthe week.\n\nAEH",
